matrix $A$"] ["text" "${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/state.json" "file_sha256" "138f05f1511971e6558c22b2b6acb9a020147b7e3bb50e15f40b2fc8c6e97132"} {"question" "q-336639" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/state.json" "file_sha256" "138f05f1511971e6558c22b2b6acb9a020147b7e3bb50e15f40b2fc8c6e97132"} {"question" "q-336477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/state.json" "file_sha256" "138f05f1511971e6558c22b2b6acb9a020147b7e3bb50e15f40b2fc8c6e97132"} {"question" "q-336477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Starting from $\\text{L2},$ we have the identity\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this identity at $t=0$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L3}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{evaluating both sides at }t=0.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Substituting ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$ into $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substituting }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}\\text{ into }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$, $\\text{L2},$ $\\text{L5},$ and $\\text{L6}.$ From $\\text{L1}$ we have ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At},$ and from $\\text{L5}$ we know $c=x(0).$ Substituting ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ from $\\text{L1}$ and $c=x(0)$ from $\\text{L5}$ into $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ (line $\\text{L2}$), we obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and substitution from $\\text{L1}\\text{ and }\\text{L5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/state.json" "file_sha256" "138f05f1511971e6558c22b2b6acb9a020147b7e3bb50e15f40b2fc8c6e97132"} {"question" "q-336477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d27dde9e4f0f4bbbb77fb9930440ee466a7e9927dac3075f1b781d29f35db3"} {"question" "q-336477" "field" "