\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*"] [17592186229632 :question/answer-fields 17592186228219] [17592186229624 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-4y(t); y^{′}(t) = 4x(t)$\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) & -\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t) & \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*"] [17592186229624 :question/answer-fields 17592186228183] [17592186229637 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-3y(t),\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} & te^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.*"] [17592186229652 :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix $Φ_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n$Φ_{0}^{′}(t) = AΦ_{0}(t), Φ_{0}(0) = I_{n}$.\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(t_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $Φ_{0}(t) = e^{At}$\n> $L2$: $x(t) = Φ_{0}(t)c$ for some constant vector $c$\n> $L3$: $x(t) = e^{At}c$\n> $L4$: $x(t_{0}) = e^{At_{0}}c$\n> $L5$: $(e^{At_{0}})^{-1}x(t_{0}) = (e^{At_{0}})^{-1}e^{At_{0}}c$\n> $L6$: $c = e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0})$\n> $L7$: $x(t) = e^{At}e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0})$\n> $L8$: $x(t) = e^{At - At_{0}}x(t_{0})$\n> $L9$: $x(t) = e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0})$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ is true by {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from $L3$ {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L8$ follows from $L7$ by the fact that {{field-3}}."] [17592186229652 :question/answer-fields 17592186228333] [17592186229652 :question/answer-fields 17592186228337] [17592186228862 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-7x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-7y(t)$\nFind the general solution to the system of ODEs above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$*"] [17592186228862 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= Φ_{0}(t)c \\\\\n&= e^{At}c \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}te^{-7t} \\\\ e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{-7t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-7t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$."] [17592186228870 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 3y(t); y^{′}(t) =-3x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*"] [17592186228870 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{x}(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}x(0) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (3t) - \\sin (3t) \\\\ -2\\sin (3t) - \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228869 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 7y(t); y^{′}(t) = 7x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7t) & \\sinh (7t) \\\\ \\sinh (7t) & \\cosh (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*"] [17592186228869 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\mathbf{c} =e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t = t_0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nand since $e^{At}$ is invertible for all $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $\\mathbf{x}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0}) \\\\\n&= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{A(t - 1)}x(1) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7(t - 1)) & \\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ \\sinh (7(t - 1)) & \\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ 3\\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228863 :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n$\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\qquad \\Phi_0(0) = I_n$.\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi_0(t)=e^{At}$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi_0(t)\\,\\mathbf{c}$ for some constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{At}\\,\\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $(e^{A t_0})^{-1} \\mathbf{x}(t_0)=(e^{At_0})^{-1} e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{c} = e^{-A t_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} e^{-At_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At - At_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{A(t-t_0)} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n\nFill in the blanks to justify each step of the following reasoning.\n\n$\\qquad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from $\\textrm{L4}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\qquad$ The property of the matrix exponential used in $\\textrm{L8}$ is $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\qquad$ Line $\\textrm{L9}$ follows from $\\textrm{L8}$ by the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$"] [17592186228863 :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\textrm{L4}$ and $\\textrm{L5}$. Starting from $\\textrm{L4}$,\n$\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$,\nwe multiply both sides on the left by $(e^{At_0})^{-1}$ to get\n$(e^{A t_0})^{-1}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=(e^{At_0})^{-1}e^{A t_0}\\,\\mathbf{c}$,\nwhich is line $\\textrm{L5}$. Therefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from $\\textrm{L4}$ by $(e^{At_{0}})^{-1}$\n- Next, we justify the step $\\textrm{L7}$ to $\\textrm{L8}$. Since $At$ and $-At_0$ commute, we may combine exponentials:\n$e^{At}e^{-At_0}=e^{At-At_0}$.\nSubstituting this into line $\\textrm{L7}$ produces line $\\textrm{L8}$. Hence, the property of the matrix exponential used in $\\textrm{L8}$ is $X$\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L8}$ and $\\textrm{L9}$. Starting from $\\textrm{L8}$,\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{At-At_0}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$,\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-At_0=A(t-t_0)$ to get\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$,\nwhich is line $\\textrm{L9}$. Thus, $\\textrm{L9}$ follows from $\\textrm{L8}$ by the fact that $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt lightgray]{At-At_0=A(t-t_0)\\text{for any}t\\text{and}t_0}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228184 17592186228185 17592186228186 17592186228187 17592186228188 17592186228220 17592186228221 17592186228222 17592186228223 17592186228224 17592186228249 17592186228250 17592186228251 17592186228252 17592186228253 17592186228334 17592186228335 17592186228336 17592186228338 17592186228339 17592186228340 17592186228341 17592186228343 17592186228344 17592186228345] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228183 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228183 :answer-field/id] [17592186228183 :answe